IB/hfi1: fix the PIO_CRED credit-return mmap

Prepared against Linux v6.17 (e5f0a698b34ed76002dc5cff3804a61c80233a7a).
This is the complete fix.  It supersedes the two partial patches kept
alongside it for the record:
  - hfi1-pio-cred-page-offset-pointer-arith-v6.17.patch (crash only)
  - hfi1-pio-cred-map-correct-page-v6.17.patch (crash + page selection)

hfi1_file_mmap()'s PIO_CRED case must give user space the single
credit-return page that holds this context's entry.  Three things are wrong
with the way it does that.

1. Wrong node.  `uctxt->sc->hw_free` points into the credit-return
   allocation of the send context's own node: pio.c does

	sc->hw_free = &sc->dd->cr_base[sc->node].va[gc].cr[index];

   and user contexts are allocated with `sc_alloc(dd, SC_USER, ..., dd->node)`,
   so that is the HFI-local node.  The mmap code instead uses
   `dd->cr_base[uctxt->numa_id]`, the node of whichever CPU the process
   happens to run on.  On a two-socket host with the process off the
   HFI-local node the two allocations differ, so both the offset and the DMA
   handle are computed against the wrong buffer.

2. Pointer arithmetic on a byte offset.  `cr_page_offset` is a byte offset
   (0, PAGE_SIZE or 2 * PAGE_SIZE) but `.va` is a `struct credit_return *`,
   so adding it scales by sizeof(struct credit_return) == 64 and memvirt
   lands 256 KiB or 512 KiB past a 10240-byte allocation.  That address is
   inside the vmalloc range but in no vm_area, so dma_mmap_coherent() ->
   iommu_dma_mmap() finds no pages, vmalloc_to_pfn() returns
   page_to_pfn(NULL), and remap_pfn_range() installs a frame above
   MAXPHYADDR.  The first user read takes "Corrupted page table" /
   "Oops: Bad pagetable" with a reserved-bit fault.  This is intermittent:
   when the entry is on the first page the offset is 0 and the scaling is a
   no-op.

3. Wrong page even without the scaling.  dma_mmap_coherent() describes a
   whole coherent buffer and picks the page with vma->vm_pgoff; offsetting
   cpu_addr does nothing, because for a vmap'd allocation iommu_dma_mmap()
   uses cpu_addr only to locate the vm_area and then maps pages[vm_pgoff].
   User space therefore always receives the first credit-return page, every
   credit read is for the wrong context, and send PIO stalls forever.

Fix all three: resolve the credit-return buffer through the send context's
own node, keep the byte arithmetic in integers, and use the DMA API as
intended by passing the base of the allocation with its full length and
selecting the page through vm_pgoff.  A separate length is needed because
memlen must keep describing the VMA for the existing size check.  The
dma-direct path stays correct as well, since it adds the same vm_pgoff to
the base pfn.

Measured on a Dell T7610 (Xeon E5-2650 v2, 46-bit MAXPHYADDR, Intel IOMMU in
DMA-FQ mode, kernel 6.17.0-1030-oem) against a Threadripper PRO 3995WX peer
(kernel 6.17.0-35-generic), both on Omni-Path 100:

  - stock: psm2_ep_open() takes the Oops, intermittently;
  - with (2) alone: psm2_ep_open() succeeds; PSM2_SDMA=2 (send PIO disabled)
    transfers fine, PSM2_SDMA=0 (send PIO only) hangs every time;
  - with (2)+(3) but not (1): mmap of sc_credits_addr returns -ENXIO,
    because the cross-node offset exceeds the buffer;
  - with this patch: send PIO, send DMA and the default mixed mode all work.

--- a/drivers/infiniband/hw/hfi1/file_ops.c
+++ b/drivers/infiniband/hw/hfi1/file_ops.c
@@ -326,6 +326,7 @@
 	void *memvirt = NULL;
 	dma_addr_t memdma = 0;
 	u8 subctxt, mapio = 0, vmf = 0, type;
+	size_t memdmalen = 0;
 	ssize_t memlen = 0;
 	int ret = 0;
 	u16 ctxt;
@@ -372,6 +373,7 @@
 		break;
 	case PIO_CRED: {
 		u64 cr_page_offset;
+		struct credit_return_base *cr;
 		if (flags & VM_WRITE) {
 			ret = -EPERM;
 			goto done;
@@ -380,12 +382,23 @@
 		 * The credit return location for this context could be on the
 		 * second or third page allocated for credit returns (if number
 		 * of enabled contexts > 64 and 128 respectively).
+		 *
+		 * It always lives in the credit-return allocation of the
+		 * send context's own node, which is not necessarily the
+		 * node this process is running on.
 		 */
-		cr_page_offset = ((u64)uctxt->sc->hw_free -
-			  	     (u64)dd->cr_base[uctxt->numa_id].va) &
-				   PAGE_MASK;
-		memvirt = dd->cr_base[uctxt->numa_id].va + cr_page_offset;
-		memdma = dd->cr_base[uctxt->numa_id].dma + cr_page_offset;
+		cr = &dd->cr_base[uctxt->sc->node];
+		cr_page_offset = ((u64)uctxt->sc->hw_free - (u64)cr->va) &
+				 PAGE_MASK;
+		/*
+		 * dma_mmap_coherent() takes the whole coherent buffer and
+		 * selects the page with vm_pgoff, so pass the base and the
+		 * allocation length and let vm_pgoff pick the page.
+		 */
+		vma->vm_pgoff = cr_page_offset >> PAGE_SHIFT;
+		memvirt = cr->va;
+		memdma = cr->dma;
+		memdmalen = TXE_NUM_CONTEXTS * sizeof(struct credit_return);
 		memlen = PAGE_SIZE;
 		flags &= ~VM_MAYWRITE;
 		flags |= VM_DONTCOPY | VM_DONTEXPAND;
@@ -567,7 +580,8 @@
 		ret = 0;
 	} else if (memdma) {
 		ret = dma_mmap_coherent(&dd->pcidev->dev, vma,
-					memvirt, memdma, memlen);
+					memvirt, memdma,
+					memdmalen ? memdmalen : memlen);
 	} else if (mapio) {
 		ret = io_remap_pfn_range(vma, vma->vm_start,
 					 PFN_DOWN(memaddr),
